Durarara!! AU
Chapter 14 – Part 1
Curtis watched the rideshare disappear down the street, then turned on his heel and started walking back into the deeper current of the city. He stretched his arms out loosely at his sides, long black braids shifting across his back, and spoke to the night like it might answer.
“What does the city hold for me tonight?”
The low growl of a familiar engine rolled up beside him.
The Black Rider.
Celty’s yellow helmet turned toward him as the motorcycle slowed to match his pace. Curtis glanced over and gave a small, easy nod.
“Oh. Hey.”
Without ceremony, Celty reached into an inner pocket of her riding suit and held something out to him.
The photograph.
The old image of two smiling boys—one of them unmistakably a younger Curtis, the other Justice—sat between her gloved fingers. Curtis’s mismatched eyes lit up the moment he recognized it. He took the photo carefully, almost reverently, and let out a quiet breath.
“I was going to come get this back. Thank you.”
Celty lifted her PDA and typed.
Who’s Justice?
Curtis looked at the message, then at the sealed yellow helmet, and gave a small shake of his head.
“Oh no. You and your boyfriend and Shizuo all cornered me last time.” He slipped the photo safely into his jacket. “I’m not doing that again on a sidewalk.”
Inside her helmet, Celty’s thoughts flickered sharply.
Of course he’s going to not tell me—
“Maybe next time,” Curtis said mildly, answering the thought as if she had spoken it aloud, “we can do it proper over coffee.” He glanced sideways at her. “Is Shinra home? I’ll come for a visit.”
Celty’s posture stiffened on the bike.
The yellow helmet snapped toward him fully. For a second she forgot the PDA entirely, the usual careful distance cracking under the jolt of being overheard again.
She didn’t type.
She spoke—directly, in the private channel only he seemed able to hear—voice tight with startled disbelief.
“You can still hear me.”
A pause, sharper.
“You’re doing it right now.”
Curtis’s expression flattened the moment the familiar tension rose between them. He exhaled through his nose and already started to turn away.
“Here we go. You’re just fucking with me like last time.” His voice stayed even, but the edge underneath was clear. “I’m going to walk away again.”
Celty’s helmet tracked him as he moved. For a second she almost reached for the PDA out of pure habit—then stopped. There was no point pretending the usual rules still applied with him.
Her real voice came through the private channel, sharper and more frustrated than before.
“I’m not fucking with you.” A short, tight pause. “You’re the only person who has ever heard me without the PDA. Do you have any idea how long it’s been since someone could just… answer me? And every time I try to understand it, you treat it like a joke and leave.”
The yellow helmet tilted slightly.
“I’m not cornering you. I’m asking. Stay. Or at least tell me why you can hear me before you disappear again.”
Curtis stopped mid-step and tilted his head, long black braids shifting as he visibly weighed the question. For a moment it looked like he might actually answer.
Then the corner of his mouth lifted.
“Hmmm… nope!” He gave a small, unapologetic smile. “I’m not telling you.”
The refusal landed light, almost playful—completely different from the defensive wall he had thrown up the last time. He rocked back on his heels slightly, mismatched eyes half-lidded with mild amusement, as if the act of withholding the answer was itself the point.
Celty stared at him through the yellow helmet.
The private channel was silent for a full second. Then her voice came through, flat with disbelief and rising exasperation.
“…You’re smiling about it.”
Another pause.
“You can hear me when no one else in this city can. You answered me in front of Shinra and Shizuo. You just did it again. And your response is to smile and say ‘nope’?”
The helmet tilted a fraction sharper.
“You are the most frustrating person I have met in years.”
Curtis gave a small shrug, as if the conversation had reached its natural end, and turned to walk away.
Celty moved faster.
Black shadow erupted from beneath her riding suit, coiling through the air and snapping tight around his wrist with unnatural strength. The tendril locked in place, holding him there on the sidewalk under the streetlights.
“I’m not done talking to you.”
Curtis looked down at the shadow binding his wrist, then up at the yellow helmet. His voice dropped, calm but edged.
“Don’t do that. I’m warning you.”
Celty’s grip tightened. The shadow didn’t yield.
“No one has been able to hear me for as long as I can remember.” Her real voice came through the private channel, stripped of the usual restraint. “Years. Decades. And then you show up and answer me like it’s nothing. Give me something.”
Curtis’s mismatched eyes narrowed. He didn’t pull yet, but the air around him had gone heavier.
“I don’t need to give you anything. Now let go.”
“No.”
The word was flat. Final. The black shadow around his wrist flexed once, holding firm, as Celty kept the yellow helmet locked on him—unwilling to lose the only person who had ever heard her and simply walked away.
Black shadow coiled tighter around Curtis’s wrist as Celty reached back with her free hand. The familiar weight of the scythe formed in a surge of darkness, the long blade catching the streetlight as it solidified. She brought it around in a controlled arc, not yet striking, but clearly present—an unmistakable line in the air between them.
Curtis didn’t flinch.
He looked at the scythe the same way he had looked at knives, boards, and fists since arriving in the city: unimpressed. His mismatched eyes stayed steady on the yellow helmet.
“If you’re going to pull something out like that, make sure to use it.” His voice remained even, almost bored. “You’re not getting any info from me.”
The shadow around his wrist flexed once. Celty’s posture stiffened on the bike, the scythe held firm in her grip. For a long second the only sound was the low idle of her motorcycle and the distant noise of the city.
Then her real voice came through the private channel—quieter than before, but edged with something sharper than frustration.
“…You’re not even afraid of it.”
A pause.
“Most people run when they see this. Or freeze. Or beg. You look at it like it’s a prop.” The yellow helmet tilted slightly. “You hear me. You answer me. You stand there with my scythe in your face and still refuse to give me a single real answer.”
The shadow around his wrist didn’t loosen.
“Why are you like this?”
Curtis met the sealed yellow helmet without blinking.
“Because you don’t scare me.” His voice stayed low, almost conversational. “My worst fears have already come true. Everything else is trivial.”
A short pause. The corner of his mouth lifted slightly.
“And plus… you’re the third Dullahan I’ve met in my life.”
Something in Celty snapped taut at the casual way he said it.
The shadow around his wrist clenched harder. She swung.
The scythe came around in a full, vicious arc—black blade cutting the air with lethal speed—only to stop dead a hair’s breadth from the side of his neck. The edge hovered there, close enough that the faintest movement would have drawn blood. The force of the halted swing still hummed in the metal.
Curtis didn’t pull back.
Instead, he leaned in and pressed a light, deliberate kiss against the flat of the scythe’s blade.
When he spoke again, his breath ghosted across the black steel.
“Please continue.” His mismatched eyes lifted to the yellow helmet, calm to the point of emptiness. “You honestly think I’m afraid to die? I’m not.”
The night seemed to hold its breath around them.
Celty’s real voice came through the private channel in a tight, shaken rush—anger, disbelief, and something closer to alarm tangled together.
“…You’re insane.”
The scythe remained locked at his throat, unmoving. The shadow around his wrist trembled once with the effort of restraint.
“You kiss a reaper’s blade and ask for more. You talk about Dullahans like they’re ordinary. You stand there and invite it.” A sharp, uneven pause. “What happened to you that death feels like nothing?”
Curtis’s free hand closed around the black shadow binding his wrist.
For a second the darkness resisted—then he twisted and snapped it. The tendril tore apart like brittle smoke and dissipated into the night air. He rolled his wrist once, as if the restraint had been nothing more than an annoyance, and took a half-step back from the scythe still hovering near his neck.
“A lot has happened,” he said flatly. “I’m not interested in talking about it.”
He looked at the yellow helmet, mismatched eyes steady.
“But if you want to know why I can hear you… first tell me where your head is, and why you’re not speaking through it.” A short pause. “Dullahans always have their heads.”
The question landed heavy and precise.
Celty went very still. The scythe remained raised, but the aggressive tension in her posture fractured into something more complicated—surprise, wariness, and the sharp discomfort of being seen too accurately by someone who should not have known enough to ask.
Her real voice came through the private channel quieter than before, stripped of some of its earlier anger.
“…You really have met others.”
A longer silence stretched between them under the streetlights.
“My head was stolen,” she said at last. The words were plain, almost blunt. “Years ago. I don’t know where it is. I search for it. That’s why I’m in this city. That’s why I take the jobs I take.”
The yellow helmet tilted slightly.
“I can’t speak through it because it isn’t here. The PDA is what I have left. Until I find it… this is all I am.”
A pause, heavier.
“Now you know. Why can you hear me?”
Curtis slipped his hands into his pockets, the scythe and the broken shadow already behind him in more ways than one.
“I was exposed to a heavy amount of anomalies in Appalachia,” he said evenly. “One of which granted me the ability to interact with fae, cryptids, spirits, and cursed objects.”
He left it there. No name for the woman in the forest. No mention of the brand. No reference to the presence that had made Saika recoil. Just the practical outline of why a headless rider’s voice could reach him when it reached no one else.
Celty remained still on the motorcycle, yellow helmet fixed on him. The scythe slowly lowered, the black blade dissolving back into shadow as the immediate threat drained out of the moment.
Her real voice came through the private channel quieter, more thoughtful than before.
“…Appalachia.” A pause. “That explains the way you look at things that should frighten people. And why my voice doesn’t stop at the helmet with you.”
Another beat of silence stretched under the streetlights.
“You talk about fae and cryptids the same way most people talk about weather. Like they’re ordinary.” The helmet tilted slightly. “Most humans who encounter even one of those things break. Or run. Or spend the rest of their lives trying to forget. You collected the ability and kept walking.”
A longer pause.
“…Is that all you’re willing to say?”
Curtis exhaled through his nose and gave a small, almost tired shrug.
“What more do you want? I don’t exactly have the answer for where your head is.” He glanced at her, mismatched eyes calmer than before. “But if you just want to talk… I guess we can do that. It must be rough having to use your phone or a PDA to talk to people.”
The scythe was already gone. The aggressive tension in Celty’s posture had thinned into something more complicated—wariness still present, but no longer aimed like a blade.
For a moment she simply sat there on the idling motorcycle, yellow helmet turned toward him under the streetlights. When her real voice came through the private channel again, it was quieter than it had been all night.
“…It is.”
A short pause.
“People get used to the typing. Some of them even forget there’s a person behind it. Shinra never did. But most of the city only ever sees the helmet and the screen.” Another beat. “You’re the first one in a long time who answered the voice instead of the text.”
She looked down at the handlebars for a second, then back at him.
“I don’t need you to solve the head. Not tonight. But… yes. If you’re actually willing to talk, without running off the second it gets inconvenient… I’d like that.”
The private channel stayed open between them, fragile and rare, as the night air moved through the street.
Celty was quiet for a stretch of the street before she spoke again. When she did, the private channel carried something heavier than curiosity.
She told him about the experiments.
About Shingen Kishitani cutting her open. About Shinra growing up around her body as a subject before he ever became the man who claimed to love her. About the way Shinra still looked at her sometimes—not only as a person, but as a miracle, a specimen, a fascination that had never fully separated itself from affection. She did not dress it up. She simply laid the history out the way someone does when they have lived inside it long enough for it to feel normal.
Curtis stopped walking.
The look he gave her was cold in a way he had not shown her before—not angry, not theatrical. Just stripped of the earlier dry amusement and patience. Without a word, he turned and started down the sidewalk again.
Celty’s shadow flicked out on reflex, catching his sleeve.
“What?”
Curtis looked back over his shoulder, mismatched eyes flat.
“That’s not a love story, Celty.” His voice was quiet and absolute. “That’s a one-way road of being manipulated in love.”
The words landed clean and hard.
For a moment the private channel was completely silent. The shadow around his sleeve loosened, then fell away entirely. Celty remained where she was on the motorcycle, yellow helmet turned toward his back, the truth of the statement sitting between them without defense.
Curtis did not wait for a reply.
He kept walking, long black braids shifting against his jacket as he put distance between himself and the Black Rider, leaving her alone with the shape of a relationship she had never quite allowed herself to name that clearly.
Elsewhere, the door to Shinra’s apartment received an unexpected knock.
When he opened it, a woman of the same age stood in the hallway—pink hair neatly kept, sharp eyes, and the composed posture of someone who belonged in a hospital corridor as easily as a living room. Dr. Mei, an old classmate from his medical days, smiled the moment she saw him.
“Shinra. You still answer the door like someone who only half expects company.”
Shinra’s face lit up with genuine delight.
“Mei! This is a surprise.” He stepped aside immediately, already ushering her in. “Come in, come in. You look well. Still terrifyingly competent, I assume?”
They fell into easy conversation with the kind of familiarity that suggested old late-night study sessions, shared surgical rotations, and a history that had never fully cooled. Their laughter came a little too readily. The way Shinra leaned in when she spoke, and the way she touched his arm when making a point, sat just a fraction closer than simple classmate warmth.
The apartment, for the moment, felt brighter—and more complicated—than usual.

Back on the street, Celty’s real voice snapped through the private channel.
“You don’t know what you’re talking about!”
Curtis stopped and turned, long black braids shifting across his back. His expression remained flat.
“Okay. Don’t shout.” A short pause. “Even if I can only hear you, that’s loud.”
Celty’s helmet stayed locked on him. The earlier hurt from his judgment still sat sharp beneath her words, but the volume dropped into something tighter and more controlled.
“…You don’t get to summarize my life in one sentence and walk off like you’ve solved it,” she said. “You weren’t there. You didn’t live through the experiments, or the years after, or the way he stayed when no one else did. You don’t know what it looks like from inside.”
A heavier beat passed between them under the streetlights.
“You called it manipulation. Maybe parts of it are. But it’s also the only place I’ve had for a very long time. So don’t reduce it to something that simple and then leave.”
Curtis didn’t raise his voice. He simply looked at her under the streetlights, mismatched eyes steady and unsentimental.
“That’s sad. To be manipulated into believing only one spot existed for you.”
“Stop!”
Celty’s real voice cracked through the private channel, sharp enough to feel like a flinch. The yellow helmet remained fixed on him, posture rigid on the motorcycle.
Curtis exhaled through his nose and kept going, calmer than the moment deserved.
“Why did you come see me? Why? Because I can hear you?” A short pause. “We clearly don’t share the same views.”
The question sat between them, stripped of softness.
Celty was silent for a long second. When she finally answered, the anger was still there, but underneath it was something more raw and less defended.
“…Because you’re the first person in years who could hear me without a screen,” she said. “Because when you answer, it’s me you’re answering—not the Black Rider, not the courier, not the headless curiosity people talk about. Just me.”
A tighter breath moved through the channel.
“I didn’t come looking for someone who would approve of my life. I came because for once I didn’t have to translate myself into text to be heard. And yes—we don’t share the same views. That much is obvious. But that doesn’t mean the hearing stops mattering.”
The yellow helmet lowered a fraction.
“You don’t have to agree with me. You don’t even have to like what I stay for. But don’t act like the only reason I’m here is because I’m too blind to walk away.”
Curtis was quiet for a moment after her words settled. Then he stepped forward, closed the remaining distance, and carefully put his arms around her.
The yellow helmet rested against his shoulder as he held her. His voice came lower than before, stripped of the earlier edge.
“…I’m sorry for the things I say.” A short pause. “Justice used to tell me I’m too blunt for my own good. That people are better off not knowing the truth sometimes.”
He didn’t try to take the previous words back. He simply offered the apology for the way they had been delivered, steady and unadorned.
Celty stiffened at first—old instinct, old caution. Then, slowly, her arms came up and held him in return. The tension in her shoulders did not vanish, but something in her settled against the contact anyway. For reasons she could not immediately name, the hold felt steady. Safe in a way that had nothing to do with answers or arguments.
Her real voice came through the private channel quieter than it had been all night.
“…You’re still blunt,” she murmured. “And you’re still wrong about some of it.”
A longer pause. She did not pull away.
“But… thank you. For apologizing. And for not disappearing the second it got ugly.” Another faint breath. “Most people either tiptoe around me or treat me like a problem to solve. You just said the worst version of what you thought and then stayed long enough to regret the delivery.”
Her gloved hands rested lightly against his back.
“…This is strange. I don’t usually… feel comfortable like this.”
Curtis’s voice dropped even lower, almost lost against the side of her helmet.
“…You feel like home.”
The words left him without armor—simple, uncalculated, and heavier than anything else he had said on that street.
Celty went still in his arms for a heartbeat. Then her grip tightened, gloved hands pressing more firmly against his back as she held onto him like the contact itself might disappear if she loosened it.
Her real voice came through the private channel soft and unsteady.
“…Don’t say things like that so easily.”
A longer silence stretched between them under the streetlights. She did not let go. If anything, she pulled him a fraction closer, the yellow helmet resting more fully against his shoulder.
“…I don’t remember the last time someone felt like that to me,” she admitted. “Or the last time I felt like that to someone else. It’s dangerous, Curtis. Home is something people leave. Or lose.”
Even as she said it, her arms stayed locked around him, unwilling to release the rare, quiet gravity of the moment.
Curtis’s arms stayed loosely around her, his voice quiet against the side of the yellow helmet.
“Appalachia was my home. I guess you can tell from the photo.” A short, heavier pause. “Justice isn’t around anymore. I haven’t gone home since he died.”
He exhaled slowly, the admission settling between them without decoration.
“But your presence… I gotta admit. Being this close to you makes me feel like I can almost wander the forest again.”
Celty’s grip on him tightened further for a moment, then eased into something steadier, less startled. The private channel stayed open, her real voice lower than before, stripped of the earlier defensiveness.
“…That’s a cruel thing to put on someone,” she said softly. “Making them feel like a place you lost.”
A longer silence stretched under the streetlights. She did not pull away.
“…I’m sorry about Justice.” The words were simple, but they carried weight. “And I’m sorry the forest closed for you when he did.”
Her gloved hand rested more firmly against his back.
“I don’t know if I can be that for anyone. Home. Or a path back to it. I’ve been searching for my own missing piece for so long that I forget what it feels like to be that kind of presence for someone else.” A quiet breath moved through the channel. “But… I’m glad that, for a moment, I remind you of something that wasn’t only pain.”
She stayed there in his arms, the night air cool around them, neither of them in a hurry to break the contact.
Celty remained in the quiet of the hold for another moment before her real voice came through the private channel again, softer and more careful than before.
“…Do you have a girlfriend?”
Curtis actually stumbled a half-step back, the question landing hard enough to break some of the stillness. His mismatched eyes widened a fraction as he caught his balance, long black braids shifting across his shoulders.
“Er—no.” He cleared his throat once, clearly less composed than usual. “I did have one back in California, but… let’s just say she’s really cruel.”
The admission sat awkwardly in the air between them, stripped of his usual flat control.
Celty’s yellow helmet tilted slightly as she watched him recover. When she answered, there was no teasing in it—only a quiet, measuring interest.
“…Cruel how?” A short pause. “You don’t have to answer if you don’t want to. But you said it the same way you talk about the things that left scars. Like it wasn’t a normal kind of ending.”
She didn’t step back into his space immediately. She simply stood there under the streetlights, giving him room while the private channel stayed open between them.
Curtis gave a short, humorless breath and rubbed the back of his neck, the earlier composure still only half-recovered.
“Ah. You know. Cheated on me. Manipulated me. The works.” He shrugged, mismatched eyes drifting toward the street. “There’s nothing about that part of my life that is fascinating. Just a forest boy who let a girl play with his heart.”
A dry, almost self-mocking sound left him.
“Oh dear. If Justice wasn’t there, I would probably still let her string me along. Ahah.”
The laugh didn’t have much warmth in it.
Celty watched him through the yellow helmet in silence for a moment. When her real voice came through the private channel again, it was quieter, stripped of judgment.
“…That doesn’t make you foolish,” she said. “It makes you someone who wanted to believe the best version of a person. Some people take advantage of that. The fact that you needed someone like Justice to pull you out of it doesn’t make the wound smaller.”
A short pause.
“I’m sorry she treated you that way.” Another beat, softer. “And I’m glad you weren’t completely alone when it happened. Even if talking about it still makes you laugh like it belongs to someone else.”
She remained where she was under the streetlights, the private channel open, offering neither pity nor pressure—only the rare, steady attention of someone who had also been shaped by people who loved in damaging ways.
Back at the apartment, the conversation between Shinra and Dr. Mei had long since abandoned anything resembling professional distance.
A sharp laugh rang out as Mei twisted, catching Shinra off-balance and sending them both half-sprawling across the couch in a familiar, practiced tangle. He protested through a grin; she only pressed the advantage, pink hair falling forward as she pinned his shoulder with the easy confidence of someone who had done this before. Their wrestling was light, playful, and far too comfortable—an old rhythm neither of them seemed interested in breaking. The white coat had been discarded over a chair. The earlier catch-up had clearly become something looser and closer the moment they ran out of polite updates.
Neither of them was thinking about Celty.

On the street, Curtis studied the yellow helmet under the streetlights, the earlier vulnerability still lingering between them but no longer completely unguarded.
“…So, Celty.” His voice was calmer again, curious rather than sharp. “Why ask me that question? That doesn’t have much to do with why I can hear you.”
Celty remained still for a moment. When her real voice came through the private channel, it carried a quieter, more complicated honesty than before.
“…Because hearing someone is intimate,” she said. “Whether I want it to be or not. You’re the only person who can answer my actual voice. That already puts you closer than almost anyone in this city.”
A short pause.
“And when someone starts feeling like home—even for a moment—it’s hard not to wonder who else already has a claim on them. I wasn’t trying to interrogate you. I just… wanted to know if I was stepping into a space that already belonged to someone else.”
The yellow helmet tilted slightly.
“It may not be related to the ability. But it is related to why I stayed instead of riding away after you snapped my shadow.”
Curtis actually blushed.
The reaction was small but unmistakable—a faint warmth rising under his skin as he looked away for half a second, long black braids shifting with the movement. His usual flat composure cracked just enough to show he had not expected the word intimate to land that directly.
“Oh—er. I didn’t realize hearing you was intimate.” He cleared his throat once, mismatched eyes flicking back toward the yellow helmet. “I… I don’t know how to respond. No one has a claim on me. Or however you say it…”
The sentence trailed off, unfinished and awkward in a way he rarely allowed himself to be.
Celty watched him through the helmet in silence for a moment. When her real voice came through the private channel again, it was quieter, almost careful.
“…You’re blushing.”
There was no mockery in it. If anything, the observation sounded faintly surprised.
“I didn’t say it to corner you. Only to be honest. Most people never hear me at all. The ones who live closest to me still need a screen. You don’t. That already makes this different, whether either of us planned for it or not.”
A short pause stretched between them under the streetlights.
“…And for what it’s worth,” she added, softer, “I’m glad no one has a claim on you. It means the space is still yours to offer. Or not.”
She did not step closer. She simply remained there, giving him room to recover while the private channel stayed open between them
Curtis stood there with his mouth slightly open, long black braids still against his shoulders, mismatched eyes fixed on the yellow helmet like the next sentence had simply failed to form. The usual dry control he wore like armor was nowhere in sight. Whatever Celty had just said had landed cleanly enough to leave him visibly caught by it.
For a moment the street felt very quiet.
Celty’s real voice came through the private channel, softer than before and edged with a faint, surprised awareness of her own effect.
“…You’re staring.”
A short pause.
“I didn’t mean to knock the words out of you. I’m not… used to that. Most people either keep their distance or talk over me. They don’t go quiet like this.” Another faint breath moved through the channel. “If I said too much, you can tell me. I’d rather you say so than stand there looking like I just rewrote the ground under your feet.”
Even as she said it, she didn’t pull the channel closed. She simply waited, the yellow helmet turned toward him, giving him time to find whatever he was going to say next.
Curtis finally closed his mouth and gave a small shake of his head, as if physically clearing the moment away. A faint, uneven breath left him before he found something safer to say.
“…Forget about it.” His voice was quieter than usual. “You know, Shinra’s a really lucky guy.”

Elsewhere, in the apartment, the playful wrestling had slowed into something stiller.
Shinra and Dr. Mei remained close—closer than classmates, closer than old friends catching up. Her pink hair brushed near his cheek as she said something under her breath that made him smile in a way Celty rarely saw directed at anyone else. Their faces lingered a little too near, the space between them charged with a familiarity that had never fully cooled. Neither of them pulled back right away.

On the street, Celty went quiet at Curtis’s words.
The yellow helmet remained turned toward him, but the private channel carried a more complicated silence than before. When she finally spoke, her voice was careful, stripped of the earlier softness that had left him speechless.
“…Lucky,” she repeated.
A short pause.
“He has a home. A place that doesn’t change. Someone who stayed.” Another beat stretched under the streetlights. “Most days I tell myself that’s enough. That being chosen once, even imperfectly, is more than a lot of people get.”
Her gloved hands rested against the motorcycle’s handlebars.
“…Hearing you say it out loud makes it sound simpler than it feels.”
Curtis looked down the street for a moment, long black braids shifting as a quieter honesty settled into his voice.
“I find it easier to wander. It’s peaceful. Some people find it lonely.” A short pause. “It only got lonely when Justice… well. It’s lonely without him with me anymore.”
He glanced back at the yellow helmet.
“But I’m glad I make you feel heard and seen.”
Before the weight of the admission could thicken too much, he reached into his pocket, pulled out his phone, and held it out toward her.
“Can I have your number?”
Celty was still for a second. Then she took her PDA, tapped through to her contact info, and held it out in return. They exchanged numbers under the streetlights without ceremony—his screen lighting up with her information, hers accepting his. The private channel remained open between them the entire time, more intimate than the simple act of trading digits should have felt.
When she spoke again, her real voice was softer than it had been all night.
“…You’re the first person in a long time who asked for this like it mattered.” A quiet pause. “Most people only want the courier. Or the Black Rider. Not the number that reaches me.”
She looked down at his contact now saved in her PDA.
“…Don’t disappear after this. If you’re going to make someone feel heard, you don’t get to become another voice that only shows up once.”
Another fainter breath moved through the channel.
“Thank you, Curtis. For staying long enough to ask.”
They parted without ceremony.
Celty kicked the motorcycle back to life, the black engine rolling low under the streetlights as she pulled away into the deeper current of Ikebukuro. Curtis watched the yellow helmet disappear between the neon and the dark, one hand still loosely in his pocket where her number now lived beside the photo of Justice. Then he turned the other way and started walking, long black braids shifting against his back, the city opening up in front of him the way it always did when he had nowhere specific to be.
From a distance, it would have looked ordinary. Two people exchanging a few words on a sidewalk and going their separate ways.
It wasn’t.
Something had been planted in the space between them—quiet, unfinished, and already more complicated than either of them had planned for. The same way something had been planted with Rio the night he caught her off a rooftop and later made her tea in a borrowed evening. The same way something had been planted with Anri under an overpass when he offered his arm to a cursed blade and then walked her toward the light.
Curtis Nightstar had a habit of treating people carefully.
He listened when others talked over them.
He stayed when it would have been easier to leave.
He offered safety without demanding a price for it.
It made people look at him longer than they meant to.
It made them save his number.
It made them wonder, in the soft hours after, whether the space beside him was still empty.
A boy watching the city from far enough away might have said it plainly:
If he kept going like this—if he kept being kind without calculating the cost—then Rio, Anri, and Celty would not remain separate threads for long. They would start to pull against each other around the same quiet center. And Curtis, who only ever seemed to understand the danger after it had already sat down beside him, would one day look up and realize he had made himself the kind of problem that soft hearts and lonely people create together.
The city did not care.
It only kept moving, carrying all of them forward into the night.
In the apartment, the playful closeness had tipped past the point of deniability.
Shinra and Mei were tangled on the couch, mouths meeting with the kind of familiar urgency that said this was not the first time old tension had boiled over. Her pink hair spilled across his shoulder as he kissed her back, hands steady at her waist—
—then he pulled away suddenly, breath uneven.
“We can’t.” His voice was low, strained with equal parts desire and caution. “Celty could be home any minute.”
Mei stayed close for a second longer, then let out a soft, knowing breath and sat back. She smoothed her hair with practiced ease, eyes half-lidded with amusement rather than offense.
“I figured you were still with the headless woman…” A small smile curved her mouth. “But I still had fun.”
Shinra’s own smile returned, quieter and a little guilty around the edges.
“I did too.”
They stood, straightened clothes, recovered discarded composure, and moved into the main room as if the previous minutes had been nothing more than an intense conversation. By the time they settled into the chairs near his desk, the apartment looked ordinary again—papers in place, white coat over the chair, the air only slightly warmer than it should have been.
The door opened.
Celty stepped inside, yellow helmet turning first toward Shinra, then toward the unfamiliar pink-haired woman. She gave a small nod of acknowledgment and closed the door behind her, the private weight of her conversation with Curtis still sitting quietly under the surface of her thoughts.
She did not suspect what had just ended on the couch.
Shinra, for his part, rose with his usual bright welcome and began introductions, completely unaware that a different fracture was already forming elsewhere—that a tall foreigner with mismatched eyes and a blunt tongue had begun to occupy a space in Celty’s chest that had, for years, belonged almost entirely to the man now smiling across from her.
The apartment held all three of them in the same warm light.
None of them could see the full shape of what was already in motion.
Mei turned toward the doorway with an easy, practiced smile, pink hair falling neatly over one shoulder as she regarded the familiar yellow helmet.
“Celty. So good to see you again.”
Celty paused just inside the apartment, the residual quiet of her conversation with Curtis still sitting under the surface. She lifted her PDA and typed with clean, efficient movements.
Mei. When did you get back in the city?
Mei’s smile deepened a fraction, warm and uncomplicated on the surface.
“Only this evening,” she answered lightly. “I had some business in Tokyo and thought I’d see if Shinra was still living in the same place. He is, of course. Some things never change.”
She glanced sideways at Shinra with a small, private spark of amusement before looking back to Celty.
“I hope I didn’t interrupt anything important. You look like you just came in from a long ride.”
Celty typed a short reply and turned the PDA so they could see it.
Yeah. A little tired. I’m going to go to my room for a bit.
Shinra nodded at once, the bright host-expression still in place.
“Of course. Rest well, Celty.”
She gave a small incline of the yellow helmet toward both of them and disappeared down the short hallway. The door to her room closed with a soft, final click.
Only then did Mei lean in, voice dropping to a whisper meant for Shinra alone.
“Good call earlier. If we didn’t stop and we—”
Shinra’s face warmed immediately. He glanced toward the hallway, then back to her, the earlier composure cracking into a flustered half-smile.
“…Yeah. I know. Which is why I called it off.”
Mei only looked amused, pink hair shifting as she sat back again, the secret sitting comfortably between them in the quiet apartment.

In her room, Celty removed the helmet and set it on the desk. The familiar dark of the space settled around her as she lay down on the bed, riding suit still half on, the night’s weight finally catching up.
She stared at the ceiling for a while.
Then, without fully deciding to, she reached for the PDA.
Curtis’s newly saved number sat near the top of her contacts.
Her thumb hovered over it.
She did not open a message. Not yet. But the impulse was already there—quiet, unexpected, and stronger than she wanted to admit. The private channel from the street still felt closer than the apartment around her. Closer, in that moment, than the man in the other room who had just finished telling someone else it was good they had stopped.
Celty set the PDA down on her chest and closed her eyes.
The city kept moving outside the window.
And for the first time in a long while, the first name in her thoughts was not Shinra’s.
